            {
              "id": "glog-b778680d-3b54-423f-8ad8-adbca1b042fb",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe \"Detection of exec Usage\" vulnerability in Python arises when the `exec()` function is used to execute dynamically generated Python code. This can lead to security risks, especially if the input to `exec()` is not properly sanitized, as it may allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code. The specific vulnerability sink in the code example is:\n\n```python\nexec(func_source, {}, namespace)\n```\n\nThis usage of `exec()` can be dangerous if `func_source` contains untrusted input, potentially leading to code injection attacks.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Avoid Using `exec()`**: If possible, refactor the code to avoid using `exec()`. Consider using safer alternatives like functions, classes, or other Python constructs that do not require dynamic code execution.\n\n2. **Input Validation and Sanitization**: If `exec()` must be used, ensure that the input is thoroughly validated and sanitized. Only allow known safe inputs and reject anything that could be potentially harmful.\n\n3. **Use Restricted Execution Environments**: Limit the execution environment by providing a restricted set of built-in functions and variables. This can be done by controlling the `globals` and `locals` dictionaries passed to `exec()`.\n\n4. **Logging and Monitoring**: Implement logging and monitoring to detect any unusual or unauthorized use of `exec()` in your application.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo mitigate the risk associated with the use of `exec()`, consider the following code fix:\n\n```python\n# Example of safer alternative\ndef execute_function(func_name, namespace):\n    if func_name in namespace:\n        func = namespace[func_name]\n        if callable(func):\n            return func()\n    raise ValueError(\"Function not found or not callable\")\n\n# Usage\nnamespace = {\n    'safe_function': lambda: \"This is safe\"\n}\n\n# Instead of using exec(), call the function directly\nresult = execute_function('safe_function', namespace)\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code example does not require any additional library dependencies to execute properly, as it uses only built-in Python functionality.\n\n### Relevant Links\n\n- [OWASP Code Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Code_Injection)\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B102"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-ce6af6a6-c2d1-4734-ab1a-9f7dae63d82e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe `pyCrypto` library, including its `SHA256` module, is deprecated and unmaintained. This poses a security risk as deprecated libraries may contain vulnerabilities that are not patched. It is recommended to switch to the actively maintained `pyca/cryptography` library, which provides a more secure and up-to-date implementation of cryptographic functions.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n- Replace the use of the `pyCrypto` library with the `pyca/cryptography` library.\n- Regularly update your dependencies to ensure you are using the latest versions with security patches.\n- Review your code for any other deprecated or unmaintained libraries and replace them with supported alternatives.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the deprecated `pyCrypto` code with the following using `pyca/cryptography`:\n\n```python\nfrom cryptography.hazmat.backends import default_backend\nfrom cryptography.hazmat.primitives import hashes\n\n# Create a SHA256 hash object\ndigest = hashes.Hash(hashes.SHA256(), backend=default_backend())\n\n# Update the hash object with data\ndigest.update(b\"your data here\")\n\n# Finalize the hash and get the digest\nhash_value = digest.finalize()\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nTo execute the code example properly, the following library dependencies are required:\n\n- `cryptography`\n\nYou can install the required library using pip:\n\n```bash\npip install cryptography\n```\n\n### Relevant OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B413"
                ]
              }
